# concise, domain-aware explanations for syllabus phrases
RULES = [
('big-o','Big-O gives an asymptotic upper bound on growth; Big-Ω gives a lower bound and Big-Θ gives a tight bound. Ignore constants and lower-order terms when describing large-input growth. Example: 3n²+5n+2 is Θ(n²).'),
('binary search','Binary search requires sorted data. Compare the target with the middle item and discard the half that cannot contain it. Repeating halves the search interval, giving O(log n) time; iterative search uses O(1) extra space.'),
('linear search','Linear search examines items sequentially until the target is found or the collection ends. It works on unsorted data. Best case is O(1), worst case O(n), and it is useful for small or unsorted collections.'),
('stack','A stack follows LIFO (last in, first out). push inserts at the top, pop removes the top, and peek reads it. Check overflow for fixed arrays and underflow before popping an empty stack.'),
('queue','A queue follows FIFO (first in, first out). Enqueue inserts at the rear and dequeue removes from the front. Circular queues reuse freed array positions; deques allow operations at both ends.'),
('linked list','A linked list stores data in nodes connected by pointers. It supports flexible insertion/deletion without shifting a whole array, but accessing the kth item requires traversal from the head (O(n)).'),
('tree','A tree is a hierarchical, connected acyclic structure. Learn root, leaf, parent, child, depth and height. Traversals visit nodes in a defined order; BST ordering enables efficient search when balanced.'),
('sort','Sorting arranges records by a key. Compare algorithms using time, auxiliary space, stability and in-place behaviour. Merge sort is O(n log n); quicksort averages O(n log n) but can degrade to O(n²).'),
('hash','A hash function maps a key to a table index. Collisions are handled by chaining or open addressing. Performance depends on a good hash function, load factor and resizing policy.'),
('graph','A graph contains vertices and edges. An adjacency matrix uses O(V²) space; an adjacency list uses O(V+E). BFS explores level by level with a queue; DFS explores deeply using recursion or a stack.'),
('osi','The OSI model has Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation and Application layers. Each layer provides services to the one above and uses the one below; encapsulation adds headers as data moves down.'),
('tcp','TCP is connection-oriented and provides reliable, ordered byte-stream delivery using sequence numbers, acknowledgements, retransmission and flow/congestion control. UDP is connectionless with lower overhead but no delivery/order guarantee.'),
('udp','UDP is a connectionless transport protocol with small headers and no built-in retransmission or ordering. It suits latency-sensitive traffic such as voice, live video, DNS and many real-time applications.'),
('ipv4','IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses, commonly written as four decimal octets. A subnet mask/prefix separates network and host bits. CIDR uses /n notation; subnetting divides an address block into smaller networks.'),
('ipv6','IPv6 uses 128-bit hexadecimal addresses and supports a vastly larger address space. Leading zeroes in a hextet may be omitted and one run of zero hextets may be compressed with ::.'),
('crc','CRC treats a bit string as a polynomial and divides it by a generator polynomial; the remainder is appended as the check value. The receiver repeats the calculation to detect many transmission errors.'),
('virtual function','A virtual function enables runtime dispatch. When a base pointer/reference refers to a derived object, the overridden derived implementation is selected at runtime. A virtual destructor is important for safe deletion through a base pointer.'),
('pure virtual','A pure virtual function is declared with = 0. A class containing one is abstract and cannot be instantiated directly. Concrete derived classes must implement the required interface.'),
('smart pointer','Smart pointers apply RAII to dynamic memory. unique_ptr provides exclusive ownership, shared_ptr shares ownership through a reference count, and weak_ptr observes a shared object without keeping it alive.'),
('move semantics','Move semantics transfer resources from a temporary or expiring object instead of copying them. A move constructor/assignment can make vector/string operations cheaper; std::move enables move overload selection but does not move by itself.'),
('mutex','A mutex protects a critical section so only one thread accesses shared mutable state at a time. Use RAII locks such as lock_guard/unique_lock and acquire multiple locks consistently to reduce deadlock risk.'),
('chain of custody','Chain of custody is the time-stamped record of who collected, handled, transferred, stored and examined evidence. Each action should identify the person, date/time, purpose and seal/identifier.'),
('hashing','A cryptographic hash maps input data to a fixed-length digest. Matching hashes before and after imaging support integrity verification. MD5 is collision-broken and should not be treated as secure authentication; SHA-256 is generally preferred.'),
('volatile','Volatile evidence can disappear when power is lost or a system changes. RAM, running processes, active network connections and logged-in sessions should generally be considered before persistent disk data.'),
('incident','A common incident-response cycle is preparation, detection/analysis, containment, eradication, recovery and lessons learned. Preserve evidence and document decisions while restoring services safely.'),
('generative ai','Generative AI learns patterns from training data and produces new content such as text, images, audio or code. A language model predicts token sequences; plausible output is not proof of factual correctness.'),
('large language model','An LLM represents text as tokens and uses a neural architecture (commonly a Transformer) to predict likely next tokens conditioned on context. Context windows limit how much input it can consider at once.'),
('prompt','A strong prompt specifies the task, context, constraints and desired output format. Add examples when useful, break complex tasks into steps, and verify facts and assumptions in the response.'),
('percentage','x percent means x per 100. To increase by r%, multiply by (1+r/100); to decrease, multiply by (1-r/100). For successive changes multiply the factors rather than adding the percentages.'),
('average','Arithmetic average = total ÷ number of observations. Weighted average = Σ(value×weight) ÷ Σ(weight). If one value changes by d, the total changes by d, which helps solve replacement questions.'),
('simple interest','Simple interest is calculated only on the original principal: SI = P×R×T/100 when R is annual and T is in years. Amount = P+SI. Keep rate and time units consistent.'),
('compound interest','For annual compounding, A=P(1+R/100)^T and CI=A−P. Interest is added to principal each period, so later interest is earned on earlier interest too.'),
('calendar','An ordinary year has 365 days (1 odd day); a leap year has 366 days (2 odd days). A leap year is divisible by 4, except century years must also be divisible by 400.'),
('clock','The minute hand moves 6° per minute and the hour hand 0.5° per minute. At H:M, the smaller angle is based on |30H−5.5M|, adjusted to at most 180°.'),
('email','A professional email has a specific subject, suitable greeting, purpose-first opening, concise body, clear action/deadline, polite close and signature. Check tone, recipients and attachments before sending.'),
('skimming','Skimming is fast reading for the overall idea and structure; scanning searches for a specific detail. Main-idea questions require the whole passage, while detail questions can use scanning.'),
('blood relation','Convert each relationship statement into a small family tree. Mark gender only when stated, track generations carefully, and never assume a relationship that the question does not provide.'),
('direction','Draw a compass and track each turn. Distinguish total distance travelled from net displacement. For shadow problems, the shadow points opposite the sun direction.'),
('cloud','Cloud computing provides on-demand shared computing resources over a network. IaaS supplies infrastructure, PaaS supplies a development platform, and SaaS supplies a ready-to-use application.'),
('iot','IoT systems connect physical devices containing sensors/actuators to networks and software. A typical flow is sensing → connectivity/gateway → processing → application/action; security and interoperability are key concerns.'),
('semiconductor','A semiconductor has conductivity between a conductor and insulator and can be controlled by doping. PN junctions form the basis of diodes; transistors act as switches/amplifiers in integrated circuits.'),
('sustainable','Sustainable technology aims to meet present needs while reducing long-term environmental and social costs. Assess energy/material use across the lifecycle, not only during operation.'),
('bioengineering','Bioengineering applies engineering principles to biological systems, devices and processes. Evaluate safety, privacy, consent, fairness and environmental effects alongside technical benefits.'),
]

def explain(topic, course, unit):
    t=topic.lower()
    for key,desc in RULES:
        if key in t:
            return desc
    # Topic-specific, concise and non-placeholder explanation grounded in its wording.
    if any(k in t for k in ['definition','meaning','characteristic','overview','fundamental','basics','introduction']):
        return f"Start with a precise definition of {topic}. Explain its purpose, the problem it solves, its main components and where it is used. In an exam answer, add one practical example and distinguish it from the nearest related concept."
    if any(k in t for k in ['application','use case','real-world']):
        return f"For {topic}, connect the concept to a practical scenario. Identify the input or need, the technology/process used, the output or benefit, and one limitation. Strong examples explain not only what is used but why it is suitable."
    if any(k in t for k in ['comparison',' vs ','difference','trade-off','limitation','challenge']):
        return f"Compare the alternatives in {topic} using the same criteria: purpose, working method, performance, cost/complexity, security or reliability, and suitable use case. End with a short conclusion stating when each option is preferable."
    if any(k in t for k in ['algorithm','method','process','workflow','steps','procedure','lifecycle']):
        return f"Present {topic} as an ordered workflow. State the preconditions, list the major steps, explain what each step produces, and mention how errors are detected or handled. Use a small worked example or flowchart in the exam."
    if any(k in t for k in ['model','architecture','layer','framework']):
        return f"Treat {topic} as a structured model. Name its components in order, explain the responsibility of each component, and show how information moves between them. Include a labelled block diagram and one use case."
    if any(k in t for k in ['ethic','legal','privacy','security','risk','responsible']):
        return f"For {topic}, discuss the objective, stakeholders and possible harm. Cover consent/authority, confidentiality, integrity, accountability, transparency and applicable policy or law. Recommend safeguards and explain how they are checked."
    if any(k in t for k in ['formula','calculation','numerical','angle','rate','factor','number']):
        return f"For {topic}, identify the given values and the quantity to find before selecting a formula. Keep units consistent, show substitutions, calculate carefully and perform a reasonableness check. Write the formula and one solved example in your revision copy."
    return f"{topic} is a syllabus concept in {course}. Learn its definition, purpose, key terms and working principle. Organise the explanation into components or steps, then add a practical example, one advantage, one limitation and a comparison with a related concept."

